{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>He who sacrifices to Elohim — shall be banned</b> [if it is] to idolatry. And it is pointed with a patach, meaning: to those elohim concerning which you were warned elsewhere. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 22:19). And Ibn Ezra explained that according to the plain sense it is not speaking to Israel, for He already warned concerning idolatry in the Second Commandment; rather, it is on account of the convert mentioned after it, for on this condition shall he dwell in our land — that he not sacrifice to his god as he had been doing. And vanity shall open his mouth, for in the Ten Commandments He warned concerning idolatry by way of prohibition, while here He specifies the punishment and the judgment that is executed upon the offender, just as He did with לֹא תִּרְצָח (\"You shall not murder\") and לֹא תִּנְאָף (\"You shall not commit adultery\"), for these are the מִּשְׁפָּטִים (judgments) that He sets before them. And behold, He made it punishable by death, for <b>יָחֳרַם</b> (\"shall be banned\") — [this means] death by the court, as it says: כָּל חֵרֶם אֲשֶׁר יָחֳרַם מִן הָאָדָם לֹא יִפָּדֶה מוֹת יוּמָת (\"Any banned thing that is banned from man shall not be redeemed; he shall surely be put to death\") (Leviticus 27:29). And He said <b>יָחֳרָם</b> because, since he sacrificed to what is banned, he is liable to be banned, in the manner of: וְהָיִיתָ חֵרֶם כָּמֹהוּ שַׁקֵּץ תְּשַׁקְּצֶנּוּ וְתַעֵב תְּתַעֲבֶנּוּ כִּי חֵרֶם הוּא (\"And you shall become a ban like it; you shall utterly detest it and utterly abhor it, for it is a ban\") (Deuteronomy 7:26). And it is possible that the verse includes within the sacrificial-offering itself as well, that all of it shall become banned, to hint that it is forbidden for enjoyment [i.e., one may derive no benefit from it]:<br><br>And the verse mentioned slaughter [זְבִיחָה], and the same applies to bowing down and to all modes of service done face-to-face [i.e., in the manner of direct worship]. But other services such as offering a drink-offering, and spreading [a libation], and embracing, and kissing, are not punishable by death when not done according to their customary manner; however, in the manner of worship in every form one is liable, and even one who exposes himself [in worship] to expose oneself [before the idol]: And the correct interpretation of the word <b>לָאֱלֹהִים</b> with a patach under the lamed is that they are the angels on high, and they are called \"אֱלֹהִים\" in many places in Scripture: אֵין כָּמוֹךָ בָאֱלֹהִים ה' (\"There is none like You among the elohim, O YHWH\") (Psalms 86:8); הוּא אֱלֹהֵי הָאֱלֹהִים וַאֲדֹנֵי הָאֲדֹנִים (\"He is the God of the elohim and Lord of the lords\") (Deuteronomy 10:17); הִשְׁתַּחֲווּ לוֹ כָּל אֱלֹהִים (\"Bow down to Him, all elohim\") (Psalms 97:7). And they are also called \"אֵלִים,\" as I have already mentioned (Ramban on Exodus 15:11). And He said: בִּלְתִּי לַה' לְבַדּוֹ (\"except to YHWH alone\"), because those who sacrifice to His angels intend thereby to do His will, that they should serve as intermediaries to draw favor from Him, and it is as though they are sacrificing to El and to His attendants; therefore He said: בִּלְתִּי לַה' לְבַדּוֹ. And there is further in this matter, by way of this approach, a profound sod [secret]; from it the matter of the offerings can be understood, and the enlightened one can know it according to his understanding from what we have written elsewhere (Ramban on Exodus 5:3), and Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 22:19) alluded to it here, and we will yet expound it in the Torah of the Priests (Leviticus 1:9), with the help of HaShem, blessed be His name forever and ever:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :register {:flags {0 ["God" "Lord"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:48.411270151Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}